\f1\fs22 It was an average building in a district of mostly lower-level management on their way up the ladder or executives on their plummet down. The major shareholder of a shell company (that covered a holding company that ventured jointly with, as it turned out, no one currently in the country or even alive for the purposes of standing at arms length from a property development concern that was a holding company for) was, after the bombing of the 
\f2\i Stickett Inn
\f1\i0 , was a businessman on a downward spiral with no hope of recovery and no parachute.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Mr Siddig?\'92 Gerry offered his hand. \'91Gerry Hardman, Commissioner of Traffic Movement and Public Security. Thank you for seeing me. I understand you must be very busy at the moment.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Ahmed Siddig declined to shake Gerry\'92s hand. \'91What do you want, Mr hard man? Shouldn\'92t be off finding out who bombed my property?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry grimaced when Siddig enunciated his surname with two distinctly separated syllables. As if he\'92d never heard that before. He pressed on. \'91It\'92s terrible what happened: you a respected Airborne, you don\'92t deserve such misfortune. There should be a law against it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That\'92s right; the taxing insurance company is refusing to pay. I was compelled by the city to demolish. All I can say is, some other holier Airborne already must have their eye on buying me out at Cloudborne prices. But right now, I own the most expensive hole in the ground in Ulyssia.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I may be of some assistance to you, Mr Siddig.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh? How? The Department of Traffic Movement and Public Security is in the market for a hole in the ground, perhaps?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry smiled like a travelling salesman. \'91Well, The Department of Traffic Movement and Public Security is in the market to 
\f2\i lease
\f1\i0  a hole in the ground.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What for?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That\'92s a matter of national security.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Siddig\'92s eyes lit up with avaricious hope. \'91National security costs extra.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It always does, Mr Siddig, but if you\'92re prepared to sharpen your pencil, I\'92m sure I could persuade your insurance company to \'85 shall we say? \'85 appreciate your contribution to the great Meritocracy of Ulyssia in a time of need.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As per the agreement, and to offer cover for the change of command, Siddig agreed to lease all the plant and equipment currently on site. Deal done, he departed on a business trip to provide some some distance and plausible deniability. Gerry\'92s team made sure any extra equipment bore the same brands and logos as the Siddig\'92s equipment, and all workers on the wore the same overalls as the previous crews. To any casual observer, everyone on the site were the same working Joes as before. No one would have noticed many of the crew were officers of the Department of Traffic Movement and Public Security, and yet others were PAPER agents.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There was one person who was not a casual observer, and who had been digesting every intel report about the bombing, including the subsequent demolition and the on-the-street incarnation of the 
\f2\i Stickett Inn
\f1\i0 . He ran face recognition software over drone footage of everyone who drank and worked in the area, and his blood ran cold when it became obvious that police officers were posing as construction workers. His curiosity and paranoia were piqued when caravans of trucks began lining up around several blocks to cart excavated dirt away, clearly from tunneling activities. And when a truck full of explosives were unloaded and carried onto the site, Agent Jones knew who was behind the clandestine operation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Jihad Man.
